From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 20:17:12 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan/runtime: name a pipeline binary by its own bytes

A pipeline binary's key is the name an application stores its bytes under and
hands back later, so binaries holding the same bytes have to share one key.
The common runtime handed out the key of the shader the binary was made from,
which names what the compile was asked for: the precompiled shaders of every
stage in the linked partition, the pipeline state and the pipeline layout.
Different requests regularly compile to the same output. Three monolithic
vertex plus fragment pipelines built over one state and one layout produced
two byte-identical fragment binaries under two different keys, because their
partitions held different shaders.

An application keyed by that holds the same bytes once per pipeline that
produced them and never reuses a copy across the pipelines that could share
it, which is the sharing the extension exists to allow. dEQP reports it as a
quality warning from
dEQP-VK.pipeline.*.pipeline_binary.dedicated.unique_key_pairs, "Multiple keys
generated for identical binaries". Narrowing the key cannot fix it: only a key
over the output is identical whenever the output is.

Hash the serialized bytes instead. The kind of binary is hashed first so a
precompiled shader and a compiled one can never collide on a key while the
pipeline cache tells them apart only by the object behind it.

The keys an application supplies through VkPipelineBinaryKeysAndDataKHR are
still carried through unchanged, and a pipeline created from binaries still
consumes them in stage order rather than by key, so nothing matches on the new
value. What changes is the entry a loaded binary leaves in the device's own
shader cache: it is named by the binary's bytes rather than by the pipeline
state that first produced them, so a pipeline later created from that state
without binaries compiles again instead of finding it. That is one internal
cache hit, given up so that one binary has one name.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/vulkan/runtime/vk_pipeline.c | 38 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/runtime/vk_pipeline.c b/src/vulkan/runtime/vk_pipeline.c
index 6b51eb64d5c29ae461a4011c86024d48490cc526..367b9691c7a1e6e7e0b3f95b810d40117a6236d1 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/runtime/vk_pipeline.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/runtime/vk_pipeline.c
@@ -4082,6 +4082,32 @@ vk_common_CmdSetRayTracingPipelineStackSizeKHR(
    ops->cmd_set_stack_size(cmd_buffer, pipelineStackSize);
 }
 
+/* The key a pipeline binary carries is the name an application stores its
+ * bytes under and hands back later, so binaries holding the same bytes have
+ * to share one key.  A shader's pipeline cache key cannot give them that: it
+ * names what the compile was asked for - every stage of the linked partition
+ * the shader belongs to, the pipeline state and the pipeline layout - and
+ * different requests regularly compile to the same output.  Hashing the
+ * serialized bytes makes identical binaries share one key by construction,
+ * which no key over the compile's inputs can.
+ *
+ * The kind is hashed first so that a precompiled shader and a compiled one
+ * can never collide on a key while the pipeline cache tells them apart only
+ * by the object behind it.
+ */
+static void
+vk_pipeline_binary_key(blake3_hash key, bool precomp,
+                       const void *data, size_t data_size)
+{
+   const uint8_t kind = precomp;
+
+   struct mesa_blake3 blake3_ctx;
+   _mesa_blake3_init(&blake3_ctx);
+   _mesa_blake3_update(&blake3_ctx, &kind, sizeof(kind));
+   _mesa_blake3_update(&blake3_ctx, data, data_size);
+   _mesa_blake3_final(&blake3_ctx, key);
+}
+
 static VkResult
 vk_create_pipeline_binary(struct vk_device *device,
                           const void *key, size_t key_size,
@@ -4121,9 +4147,9 @@ vk_create_pipeline_binary_from_precomp(struct vk_device *device,
       result = vk_error(device, VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY);
 
    if (result == VK_SUCCESS) {
-      result = vk_create_pipeline_binary(device,
-                                         precomp->cache_key,
-                                         sizeof(precomp->cache_key),
+      blake3_hash key;
+      vk_pipeline_binary_key(key, true, blob.data, blob.size);
+      result = vk_create_pipeline_binary(device, key, sizeof(key),
                                          blob.data, blob.size,
                                          alloc, out_binary_h);
    }
@@ -4148,9 +4174,9 @@ vk_create_pipeline_binary_from_shader(struct vk_device *device,
       result = vk_error(device, VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY);
 
    if (result == VK_SUCCESS) {
-      result = vk_create_pipeline_binary(device,
-                                         shader->pipeline.cache_key,
-                                         sizeof(shader->pipeline.cache_key),
+      blake3_hash key;
+      vk_pipeline_binary_key(key, false, blob.data, blob.size);
+      result = vk_create_pipeline_binary(device, key, sizeof(key),
                                          blob.data, blob.size,
                                          alloc, out_binary_h);
    }
